<DocsLayout title="Desktop application" description="Develop and package the Tauri desktop shell for Hologram Live.">
  <h1>Desktop application</h1>
  <p>The Tauri application in <code>apps/desktop</code> packages Hologram's background service and provides a focused workspace for chat, files, watched <code>.holo</code> applications, and modules. Its sidebar follows the desktop navigation model and offers remembered light and dark appearances, while a system menu-bar item reports Hologram's state and provides Open, Start, Restart, Stop, and Quit actions after the main window closes.</p>

  <h2>Run in development</h2>
  <pre><code>just tauri
# equivalent to: cd apps/desktop &amp;&amp; npm install &amp;&amp; npm run dev</code></pre>
  <p>The preparation script explicitly builds the root <code>hologram-live</code> package's <code>hologram</code> binary and copies it to Tauri's ignored, target-specific sidecar staging location before the desktop shell starts. The server source remains outside <code>src-tauri</code>.</p>

  <h2>Build a release bundle</h2>
  <pre><code>just tauri build</code></pre>
  <p>Tauri builds the web frontend, compiles the native shell, and includes the release sidecar in the platform bundle.</p>

  <h2>Watch an application directory</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Put a valid <code>hologram.json</code> and its referenced layer files in one project directory.</li>
    <li>Open <strong>Applications</strong> in the desktop sidebar and choose <strong>Add directory</strong>.</li>
    <li>Select the project directory. Hologram compiles a fat archive into its desktop cache, imports it into the local catalog, and shows the watch as <strong>Ready</strong>.</li>
    <li>Choose <strong>Run</strong> on the ready project, enter a text input, and view the application output in the inspector. The same panel remains available when selecting any runnable catalog archive.</li>
    <li>Select a catalog row to inspect the verified archive and application identities, capability request, logical layers, physical section offsets, and embedded blobs.</li>
    <li>Edit <code>hologram.json</code> or a project file. Recursive changes are debounced, then compiled and imported again. A changed archive replaces only the prior variant produced by that watch.</li>
    <li>Choose <strong>Stop watching</strong> when the development loop is finished. The final immutable catalog archive remains available until it is explicitly removed.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>To run an existing archive instead of source, choose <strong>Add .holo</strong> and select it with the native file picker. Hologram verifies and imports the archive, selects it in the catalog, and focuses its Run input. Desktop execution verifies the catalog object, downloads the immutable archive to a κ-derived application-cache path, and invokes the direct executor. This supports Wasm and the experimental Python rootfs provider without exposing cache paths to the webview.</p>
  <div class="note"><strong>Safe failure behavior</strong>A failed compile changes the watch to <strong>Failed</strong> and shows the compiler diagnostic, but it does not discard the last successful archive. Fixing the source triggers another build and returns the watch to <strong>Ready</strong>.</div>
  <p>Watch registrations are stored in the desktop application's configuration and restored after restart. Generated archives live under the desktop cache, never inside the selected project, which prevents output-triggered rebuild loops. Common dependency and build trees such as <code>.git</code>, <code>.venv</code>, <code>node_modules</code>, <code>target</code>, and <code>__pycache__</code> are ignored. The reusable registry, persistence, filtering, and debounce implementation lives in <code>crates/hologram-application-watch</code>; <code>src-tauri</code> only supplies desktop paths, fixed sidecar operations, and UI event delivery.</p>

  <h2>Command boundary</h2>
  <p>The webview can invoke these narrow application commands:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>service_start</code></li>
    <li><code>service_stop</code></li>
    <li><code>service_restart</code></li>
    <li><code>service_status</code></li>
    <li><code>modules_list</code></li>
    <li><code>history_list</code></li>
    <li><code>history_create</code></li>
    <li><code>history_get</code></li>
    <li><code>chat_send</code></li>
    <li><code>objects_list</code></li>
    <li><code>file_put</code></li>
    <li><code>file_rename</code></li>
    <li><code>object_get</code></li>
    <li><code>holo_watch_list</code></li>
    <li><code>holo_watch_add</code></li>
    <li><code>holo_watch_remove</code></li>
    <li><code>holo_catalog_list</code></li>
    <li><code>holo_catalog_inspect</code></li>
    <li><code>holo_catalog_import</code></li>
    <li><code>holo_catalog_run</code></li>
  </ul>
  <div class="note"><strong>No arbitrary shell bridge</strong>Each command maps to one fixed sidecar argument, keeping the browser-facing authority narrow.</div>
  <p><code>modules_list</code> reads Hologram's negotiated module manifest. The chat view creates and switches among durable conversation threads, then sends messages through the enabled chat module. The files view uses native open/save dialogs, uploads and renames files, lists content-addressed metadata, and downloads the stored bytes. The Applications view keeps host file/directory selection in the trusted Tauri adapter, invokes only fixed compile, import, list, inspect, verify, download, and direct-run operations, and gives the webview structured results rather than a general filesystem or shell API. Because the sidecar uses the normal Hologram configuration, the service-backed surfaces can target a local service or a future authenticated cloud endpoint; workstation path authorization remains local-only.</p>
  <p>Application View layers compile complete <code>ui/</code> directories into canonical portable bundles. The host-neutral provider and dynamic surface registry now implement validation, attachment, idempotent detachment, and explicit headless failure without depending on Tauri. Hologram Desktop does not publish its concrete surface handle yet; the next slice adds its opaque per-application/layer asset origin. ADR 018 keeps application content away from a general Tauri bridge, <code>file://</code> workstation paths, and localhost redirects.</p>
</DocsLayout>
